WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:06.300
Throughout history, we humans have been drawn
to the biggest of questions.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.410
Where do we come from?

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.200
Is there a God?

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:14.640
Do we as a species have the capacity to overcome
our differences and work together to build

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.990
a better future for us all?

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:22.660
And of course, perhaps the most profound of
these big questions: do eels have testicles?

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:28.249
OK, so maybe I’m slightly overstating the
importance of that last one - the hunt for

00:00:28.249 --> 00:00:35.200
eel gonads might not have quite the same scientific
glamour as the search for dark matter or a

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:36.859
theory of everything.

00:00:36.859 --> 00:00:41.179
But, oddly enough, it’s a question that’s
had a hell of a lot of attention over the

00:00:41.179 --> 00:00:43.260
years in the scientific community.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.390
Because, as weird as it sounds, eels lack
balls.

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:52.550
And I don’t mean that in some vague idiomatic
way, either - they don’t get all weak at

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:57.450
the fins at the thought of extreme sports
or routinely back down from fights.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:58.450
No.

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:00.260
They literally don’t have testicles.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:05.040
But if that’s the case, how on God’s green
earth do they reproduce?

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:10.230
Well, before we get onto that, what exactly
are eels?

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:14.590
While they kind of resemble snakes that have
gone into hiding underwater on account of

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:20.220
being so monumentally ugly, they’re actually
a kind of fish.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:26.860
But for multitudinous reasons they’re remarkably
different to pretty much any other fish species

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:28.640
you care to mention.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:32.040
Take European freshwater eels, for example.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:36.650
They lack scales, for a start, and can breathe
air through their skin.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:43.110
That ability allows them to survive out of
water for up to 48 hours, and they’re surprisingly

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:46.270
nifty movers on land – they can even climb.

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:51.630
They hibernate in the winter if it gets too
cold by burying themselves in mud, a trick

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:56.460
they also employ if the pond they’re living
in happens to dry out in the summer.

00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:02.250
They can live to almost 100 years of age,
their blood is poisonous, and they’re also

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:08.190
remarkably difficult to kill, and will keep
on swimming for hours even after you’ve

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:13.110
cut off their heads thanks to a decidedly
stubborn nervous system.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:16.000
All in all, eels are pretty bloody odd.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.410
And that’s before we even get to their troubling
lack of testes.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:27.180
So, how does an animal without reproductive
organs create offspring?

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:32.630
Believe it or not, this conundrum has occupied
some of humankind’s most brilliant thinkers

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:34.960
for at least a couple of millennia.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:40.640
Aristotle was probably the first to grapple
with it, but the great Greek philosopher and

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:45.610
thinker was as perplexed as anyone else by
the eels’ lack of Crown Jewels.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:51.590
Still, as the father of western logic, he
did manage to come up with a fittingly logical

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:53.430
explanation.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:03:00.400
Eels don’t have reproductive organs, according
to Aristotle, because they don’t reproduce.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:06.750
And if they don’t reproduce, he reasoned,
they must come to life through some other

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:07.750
mechanism.

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:14.960
Ultimately, he concluded eels spontaneously
come into being when rainwater mixes with

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:16.470
mud.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:23.160
Not one of his finest moments, that, but considering
he basically invented science, I think we

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:24.860
can give him a pass on this one.

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:30.470
As you’re no doubt aware, Aristotle’s
influence on science loomed large long after

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:31.640
his death.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:37.860
And for that reason, the belief eels came
to life out of nothing was widely accepted

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:43.980
as scientific fact for some two thousand years,
though others did come along to put their

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:48.319
own hypotheses forward as to exactly how the
process worked.

00:03:48.319 --> 00:03:53.020
Roman author Pliny the Elder, who died in
the aftermath of the famous eruption of Mount

00:03:53.020 --> 00:04:00.760
Vesuvius that buried Pompei in 79 AD, believed
eels came into being from the partially shed

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:03.770
skins of other eels.

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:09.130
Others felt certain eels sprouted from sea
foam or burst into life when sunlight hit

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:12.500
open stretches of water in a certain way.

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:18.689
The Scots thought eels started out life as
beetles, and the English were convinced they

00:04:18.689 --> 00:04:23.210
were born when hairs from horse tails fell
into river water.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:28.580
I know I know; nobody comes out of this with
much credit, but hey - those were simpler

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:29.580
times.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:34.550
It took a while, but eventually people began
to question whether it was really possible

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:37.840
for an animal to just come to life from nothing.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:44.020
After all, the very idea seemed to violate
the laws of nature mankind had slowly been

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:48.270
uncovering in the millennia since Aristotle
met his maker.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:52.629
One man who could firmly be considered a sceptic
was Sigmund Freud.

00:04:52.629 --> 00:04:55.500
And yes, I do mean that Sigmund Freud.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:00.809
You might be wondering what the most famous
psychoanalyst in history has to do with mankind’s

00:05:00.809 --> 00:05:06.639
hunt for eel goolies, and I’ll admit it
is a little weird to see him crop up in this

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:07.639
story.

00:05:07.639 --> 00:05:12.569
But then again, if you know anything about
Freud, you’ll know the guy was absolutely

00:05:12.569 --> 00:05:19.300
obsessed with genitals, so the fact he was
once interested in eel nuts perhaps shouldn’t

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:22.059
come as much of a surprise.

00:05:22.059 --> 00:05:27.250
In 1876, when Freud was still an enthusiastic
medical student at the University of Vienna,

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:32.319
he traveled to the city of Trieste in north
east Italy, a stone’s throw away from the

00:05:32.319 --> 00:05:33.999
Slovenian border.

00:05:33.999 --> 00:05:39.270
The purpose of the trip was simple - Freud
wanted to do something nobody else ever had:

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:42.990
get his hands on a nice set of eel balls.

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:48.430
To that end, he took himself down to the city’s
port each morning to meet the fisherman coming

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:54.020
home with their catch, from whom he would
buy as many eels as he could afford.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:57.240
Then he went back home to begin the dissections.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:01.930
Freud spent several months in Trieste, and
it’s thought he worked his way through as

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:05.629
many as 400 eels during that time.

00:06:05.629 --> 00:06:10.610
The gory task seems to have gotten to him,
too, because he sounded on the verge of losing

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:16.139
his marbles in one letter to a friend, where
he confessed: “All I see when I close my

00:06:16.139 --> 00:06:20.139
eyes is the shimmering dead tissue, which
haunts my dreams."

00:06:20.139 --> 00:06:26.300
It’s no wonder he decided to take his career
in another direction not long afterwards.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:31.490
Especially when you consider all that hard
work eviscerating eels hadn’t brought about

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:33.490
the discovery Freud was hoping for.

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:38.639
He’d found some interesting looking bits
and pieces inside the abdominal cavities of

00:06:38.639 --> 00:06:44.830
several large specimens, but there was nothing
he could definitely, conclusively claim to

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:46.569
be an eel testicle.

00:06:46.569 --> 00:06:47.909
Freud had failed.

00:06:47.909 --> 00:06:53.860
Still, he wasn’t the only one sceptical
of the stories of miraculous eel origins,

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:59.550
and ten years later in 1886, a breakthrough
came.

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:06.139
French zoologist Yves Delage had been keeping
an odd, leaf-shaped marine creature known

00:07:06.139 --> 00:07:13.419
as a leptocephalus in a tank in his lab for
several months when, quite unexpectedly, the

00:07:13.419 --> 00:07:18.050
strange little beast turned into something
else entirely.

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:23.590
Yves had observed something that would change
our understanding of eels forever.

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:31.389
You see, eels are metamorphic creatures, and
they go through 6 distinct stages in what

00:07:31.389 --> 00:07:34.869
can only be described as one hell of an odd
lifecycle.

00:07:34.869 --> 00:07:40.789
They start out as leptocephali much like the
one Yves kept in his fish tank, before turning

00:07:40.789 --> 00:07:44.050
into transparent glass eels.

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:51.710
Glass eels become elvers, elvers become yellow
eels, and yellow eels become silver eels,

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:54.419
which are basically the eel’s endgame form.

00:07:54.419 --> 00:07:59.569
The funny thing is, science had known about
all of these individual animals for a long

00:07:59.569 --> 00:08:06.179
time - fisherman had been catching them and
people had been eating them since time immemorial.

00:08:06.179 --> 00:08:07.619
Jellied eel, anyone?

00:08:07.619 --> 00:08:12.520
But it had always been assumed they were different
species entirely.

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:19.120
Nobody had ever realised they were all the
same animal at different stages of its lifecycle.

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:23.969
Although Yves witnessed the metamorphosis
first hand, it took a decade or so for his

00:08:23.969 --> 00:08:25.860
discovery to be widely accepted.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:31.400
That might be because it went against conventional
wisdom, or it may just be that people didn’t

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:33.990
take poor old Yves too seriously.

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:40.479
You see, he had a habit of making outlandish
statements, and in later life would famously

00:08:40.479 --> 00:08:45.630
claim to have ‘calculated’ that the Turin
Shroud - which some people believe is the

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:53.040
fabric Christ was buried in after the crucifixion
– had a 1 in 10 billion chance of being

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:54.040
a fake.

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:56.770
I’d really love to see your working there
Yves.

00:08:56.770 --> 00:09:01.920
Anyway, it was with the discovery of this
highly unusual life cycle that the missing

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:08.660
piece fell into place to help science finally
understand why eels lack testicles.

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:14.540
Not because they spontaneously come to life
from mud, not because they’re remarkably

00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:21.560
energetic horse tail hairs, but because they
grow reproductive organs only when they need

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:24.740
them in their final, mature form.

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:29.500
And you have to hand it to them; it does sound
like a sensible system.

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:34.430
Because, as any guy will tell you, when you
aren’t using them genitals don’t half

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:35.970
get in the way.

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:41.800
So, one of the oldest questions in the animal
kingdom had finally been put to bed.

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:46.319
But, rather annoyingly, the answer brought
with it a question of its own.

00:09:46.319 --> 00:09:51.449
Because the first stage of the eel’s life
cycle - the leptocephalus - was a marine creature

00:09:51.449 --> 00:09:53.950
- it was found only in the ocean.

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:58.490
Which neatly explained why poor old Sigmund
had never stumbled across so much as a sniff

00:09:58.490 --> 00:10:05.120
of testicle during his 400 dissections - by
the time eels reach their mature stage – which

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:10.930
can take anything from 7 to 70 years - they’ve
left our shores far behind.

00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:17.230
So, that being the case - where exactly do
all these millions of eels bugger off to each

00:10:17.230 --> 00:10:18.230
year?

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:24.389
In 1904, Danish scientist Johannes Schmidt
was asking himself the very same question.

00:10:24.389 --> 00:10:26.970
Luckily, Schmidt was a doer.

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:30.610
When he came across a question that left him
scratching his head, he didn’t just shrug

00:10:30.610 --> 00:10:32.760
his shoulders and go make a sandwich.

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:37.860
He went out and found the answer - even if
it meant dedicating his life to the task.

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:44.680
Which is why, over a period of some 19 years,
Schmidt carried out the biggest survey of

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:50.069
leptocephali ever completed before or since.

00:10:50.069 --> 00:10:54.100
He was particularly interested in their size
- and for good reason.

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:59.290
Because, as he slowly built up more and more
data, he began to realise something - the

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:05.529
closer he got to a certain point in the Atlantic
Ocean, the smaller the leptocephali he netted

00:11:05.529 --> 00:11:10.140
were, until eventually, they couldn’t get
any smaller.

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:16.699
He’d found the eel’s spawning grounds
– smack in the middle of the Sargasso Sea.

00:11:16.699 --> 00:11:22.569
Not only was that rather fitting - because
much like the creatures who go there for a

00:11:22.569 --> 00:11:28.879
bit of ‘sexual eeling’, the Sargasso itself
is a bit of an enigma - it was also pretty

00:11:28.879 --> 00:11:30.399
staggering.

00:11:30.399 --> 00:11:36.720
Because it meant European freshwater eels
were undertaking a previously unknown migration

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:41.649
of some 6000 kilometres to have their young.

00:11:41.649 --> 00:11:47.949
The Sargasso is the only sea in the world
that doesn’t have a single land border.

00:11:47.949 --> 00:11:54.740
Instead, it’s surrounded by a vortex of
ocean currents that completely encircle it

00:11:54.740 --> 00:11:56.210
on all sides.

00:11:56.210 --> 00:12:02.139
It’s a sea within a sea, if you like, and
its border currents act a bit like a plug

00:12:02.139 --> 00:12:03.360
hole for the Atlantic.

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:09.339
That’s why, as well as millions of randy
eels, it’s also home to the North Atlantic

00:12:09.339 --> 00:12:15.160
garbage patch - hundreds of square kilometres
of ocean filled with rubbish.

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:16.740
Nice one, humans.

00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:22.060
Despite Schmidt’s conclusions, which are
still accepted today, nobody has ever actually

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:25.400
seen eels mating in the Sargasso Sea.

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:32.370
In fact, even with all our fancy technology,
nobody has ever seen eels in the Sargasso

00:12:32.370 --> 00:12:34.000
at all.

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:38.800
Despite the fact we’re pretty confident
millions of them make the journey there every

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:39.800
year.

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:46.120
And in keeping with that somewhat patchy understanding
of the behaviour of our slippery friends,

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:52.860
we don’t really know how the tiny eel larvae
born there make it the 6000 kilometres back

00:12:52.860 --> 00:12:54.449
to Europe, either.

00:12:54.449 --> 00:13:00.080
Their strange, leaf-like shape is thought
to help with drifting, but some researchers

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:04.170
believe active swimming plays at least some
part in the trip.

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:09.010
We also don’t know how long the journey
takes, or even how long eels stay in their

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:15.220
larval state - estimates range from as little
as 6 months to as many as 3 years.

00:13:15.220 --> 00:13:20.720
So yeah, we may have found their balls, but
eels still seem to have plenty of other secrets

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:22.459
hidden up their sleeves.

00:13:22.459 --> 00:13:24.209
Well, sleeve, I guess.

00:13:24.209 --> 00:13:29.839
We’ve mostly been covering freshwater eels
in this video, but eels don’t get any less

00:13:29.839 --> 00:13:33.800
weird no matter which bit of the family tree
you’re looking at.

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:39.769
There are snipe eels that look a bit like
nightmarish lassos, slender moray eels that

00:13:39.769 --> 00:13:47.370
can grow to be 4 metres long, and European
congas that can weigh as much as 110 kilos.

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:53.209
Rarely seen gulper eels live at depths of
up to 3000 metres and are a confusing mix

00:13:53.209 --> 00:13:58.420
of disturbing and cute - kind of like what
might have happened if Nintendo had decided

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:01.790
to turn Kirby into a horror franchise.

00:14:01.790 --> 00:14:05.300
And then there are parasitic snubnosed eels.

00:14:05.300 --> 00:14:10.320
These disturbing little living horrors use
their powerful jaws to burrow into the bodies

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:15.260
of larger fish, where they just… make themselves
at home.

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:19.930
Typically they can be found snuggled up alongside
the spines of species like cod and halibut,

00:14:19.930 --> 00:14:25.720
but they’ve also been known to burrow directly
into the abdomen of gravid fish where they

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:31.519
promptly devour thousands of unhatched eggs
within the space of just a few minutes.

00:14:31.519 --> 00:14:37.670
In 1992, two parasitic eels were even found
to have made a cosy residence in the still-beating

00:14:37.670 --> 00:14:43.569
heart of a 400kg Mako shark caught near Montauk
in New York State.

00:14:43.569 --> 00:14:46.959
I should probably mention electric eels, too.

00:14:46.959 --> 00:14:50.980
Not because they can deliver an electric shock
of close to a thousand volts - although they

00:14:50.980 --> 00:14:56.699
can - but because they aren’t actually eels
at all - they’re a kind of knifefish.

00:14:56.699 --> 00:15:02.620
Bit of an embarrassing slip there, hey science
- although not an especially uncommon one.

00:15:02.620 --> 00:15:06.970
Killer whales aren’t whales - they’re
members of the dolphin family.

00:15:06.970 --> 00:15:13.550
American buffalo are actually bison, honey
badgers are most closely related to martens,

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:20.050
and horny toads are in fact lizards - and,
I might add, no more desperate for sex than

00:15:20.050 --> 00:15:22.009
any other animal out there.

00:15:22.009 --> 00:15:28.060
Knifefish imposters aside, eels are some of
the weirdest animals ever to have existed

00:15:28.060 --> 00:15:29.470
on planet earth.

00:15:29.470 --> 00:15:35.199
But while there are still plenty of mysteries
left to uncover about these strange and elusive

00:15:35.199 --> 00:15:40.670
creatures, we do at least finally know for
sure that, yes, they do have balls.

00:15:40.670 --> 00:15:45.490
I’m sure you’ll all sleep easier tonight
now that’s been cleared up.

00:15:45.490 --> 00:15:50.939
Which should give you plenty of time to vividly
imagine a parasitic snubnosed eel eating its

00:15:50.939 --> 00:15:55.860
way through your chest and taking up residence
in your heart.

00:15:55.860 --> 00:16:05.209
Thanks for watching.

